<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piedmont</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Piemonte</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Piedmont is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, enclosed on three sides by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> as its capital. From the 11th century the house of Savoy gained lands there, and Piedmont became the core of its state after the capital moved to Turin in 1563. As the heart of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, it led the unification of Italy in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piedmont.jpg" alt="Piedmont" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piedmont_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Piedmont is one of the 20 regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in the northwest, and the second largest in area after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> surround it on three sides, among them Monviso, where the Po rises, and Monte Rosa. The region is 43.3 per cent mountainous, 30.3 per cent hills and 26.4 per cent plain, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddies of Vercelli and Novara to the hillsides of the Langhe, Roero and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>. The name comes from the medieval Latin Pedemontium, at the foot of the mountains, attested from the end of the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Celtic-Ligurian tribes such as the Taurini and the Salassi lived there until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> subdued them about 220 BCE and founded colonies including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Augusta Taurinorum</a> (Turin) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Eporedia</a> (Ivrea). After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> the region was invaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, East Romans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and, in 773, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. In 1046 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a> added Piedmont to the County of Savoy, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, though Asti, Alessandria, Saluzzo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> stayed independent. Duke Emanuele Filiberto moved the seat to Turin in 1563, and in 1720 the Duke of Savoy became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> created client republics in Piedmont from 1796 and annexed it in September 1802; the Congress of Vienna restored the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and gave it the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> as a barrier against France. Piedmont was the springboard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> in 1859 to 1861, after unsuccessful wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1820 to 1821 and 1848 to 1849. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> was the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, from 1861 to 1865; when the capital moved the region lost administrative importance, though the Italian crown prince was still styled Prince of Piedmont, and it was one of the most important regions of early Italian industrialisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Piedmont has 4,255,006 inhabitants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> as its capital. It is a great winegrowing region, producing Barolo and Barbaresco from the Langhe and sparkling Asti, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> wine district was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2014. The Residences of the Royal House of Savoy have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> since 1997. Besides Italian, Piedmontese, Occitan, Franco-Provençal and Walser are spoken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Piedmont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1815 Austrian troops suppressed revolutions in Naples and <b>Piedmont</b> in 1821 and intervened repeatedly in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bourbons then returned under Charles II, who fled in the revolution of 1848, when Piacenza voted to join <b>Piedmont</b>; Austrian troops restored the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two years later the extinction of the Savoy-Achaea branch brought the principality of <b>Piedmont</b>, with Turin, back under the main line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the House of Savoy the island remained distinct from the much richer mainland of <b>Piedmont</b>, with its silk industry, wine and the growing city of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>With every major city a port, the kingdom owned the largest merchant fleet in the Mediterranean, yet poor interior roads hampered internal trade, and by 1859 it had only 99 kilometres of railway against <b>Piedmont</b>'s 850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the small papal army, reinforced by foreign volunteers including the papal Zouaves, was defeated by <b>Piedmont</b> at Castelfidardo in 1860 and held Rome only with French help, winning at Mentana in 1867.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
